lisa-design:
BCH-1315 — AuthZ Phase 3 (authzen HTTP driver + batch Evaluations + /me/permissions), builds on Phase 1 (#427).

- One config key by design: `authz.endpoint` is the single Access Evaluation URL; the batch
  (Evaluations) URL is DERIVED by AuthZen convention (`/evaluation` -> `/evaluations`),
  falling back to the same URL for PDPs that serve both at one path. Do not add a second
  endpoint key — the design mandates "one config key".
- Fail-mode contract lives in the HTTP error classification: transport failure / context
  timeout / 5xx / 429 wrap authz.ErrUnavailable (the PEP then applies authz.fail_mode);
  any OTHER non-2xx (4xx) is a plain error the PEP maps to 500. Rationale: a compliant
  AuthZen PDP returns its allow/deny verdict as a 200 body, so a 4xx is our bug/auth issue,
  not a deny. Don't reclassify 4xx as unavailable.
- Decision cache (authz.cache_ttl) is a GENERIC decorator (cache.go) applied in authz.Open
  when CacheTTL>0, not baked into the authzen driver. It caches only successful decisions
  (errors/unavailable never cached); key = sha256 of the FULL tuple (subject incl. props,
  action, resource incl. props, context) so nothing that changes a decision is dropped;
  Evaluations serves hits locally and batches only misses into ONE inner call; lazy expiry;
  forwards Health to the inner PDP. Default 0 = disabled (builtin gains nothing).
- /me/permissions does TYPE-LEVEL capability checks (no resource instance ID, no request
  context) over the full manifest vocabulary in a single batch Evaluations call. It honors
  fail_mode (closed -> empty permissions so the UI hides everything; open -> full
  vocabulary). Every manifest resource is always present in the response (possibly empty
  allow-list) so the UI knows the whole vocabulary. Ordering deterministic (resources
  sorted, actions in manifest order).
- Subject derivation is single-sourced: exported middleware.SubjectFromContext (was the
  unexported subjectFromContext) is reused by the handler so /me/permissions and the PEP
  build identical subjects. Don't duplicate subject logic in the handler.
- Route is `/me/permissions` (matches the UI contract in BCH-1318), deliberately NOT
  `/users/me/permissions` despite the existing /users/me group.
- /health/ready gained an OPTIONAL PDP dependency check via the new authz.Healther
  interface. builtin does NOT implement Healther (in-process, can't fail) so it's treated
  as healthy; authzen probes {origin}/.well-known/authzen-configuration with a 2s timeout.
- Scope held deliberately tight: did NOT rewrite existing list endpoints to filter via
  batch Evaluations (premature before the BCH-1319 attribute surface); the concrete batch
  consumer shipped is /me/permissions. Did NOT grow Subject/Resource attributes (BCH-1319
  owns the authoritative surface). cedar/permit/openfga, ccf authz export/sync, and audit
  logging are other phases.
